# Line endings are LF everywhere, including on a Windows checkout.
#
# Several tests in this suite read their own source and look for exact strings -
# `indexOf('\n  }\n')` to find the end of a function body, for instance. With
# git's default autocrlf on Windows those files arrive with CRLF, every one of
# those searches misses, and the failure reads `expected '' to contain ...`,
# which points at the code under test rather than at the checkout. That cost an
# afternoon the first time the suite ran on Windows, which was the day this
# project started shipping a Windows binary.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, and must not be touched by any of the above. The hardware captures in
# particular are the ground truth every driver is tested against: a single byte
# rewritten by a line-ending filter would invalidate the round-trip invariant
# and look like a decoder bug.
*.bin binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
